+## `domain-availability` (2026-09-29): a request can name a feature the sandbox structurally cannot deliver, without knowing it
+Asked for a domain-availability checker that "checks domain available" across
+many TLDs. The lab CSP's `connect-src` only reaches Bluesky's API and
+`plc.directory` — no registry/registrar/WHOIS host is reachable from a tenant
+page, so a *live* check is not a scoping choice, it's structurally impossible
+here (same shape as the `chladni-sim` Rust-in-sandbox case above, but a
+platform-imposed ceiling rather than a missing toolchain). Built a curated
+reference tool instead (real per-TLD facts, general demand reputation, no
+live signal) and said so plainly on the page itself, not just in BRIEF.md.
+Durable: when a request's core mechanic needs a network host outside the
+CSP allowlist, that's worth naming as a hard platform boundary up front
+(on-page, not just in the handoff doc) rather than quietly downgrading to
+"as close as possible" and hoping it reads as the real thing.
+
+## Said no to (2026-09-29 addition)
+Declined to add outbound links to registrar/WHOIS sites (e.g. guessing at a
+`whois.com/whois/<domain>` or a Namecheap search URL) even though such a
+plain `<a href>` would not have been blocked by the CSP — wasn't confident
+those exact URL shapes are current, and the system-level instruction not to
+guess URLs took precedence over making the page feel more complete. Worth
+knowing this requester hasn't reacted to that tradeoff yet; if they ask for
+the outbound links anyway, that's the signal to revisit it.
+
